<h1>Exercise Name: Lyng Leg Raises (specifically lower leg raise variations).</h1>
<b><h3><u>Video Overview:</u></h3></b> The 3D animation shows a model lying flat on an exercise mat with hands placed palms-down beside the hips.<br><br> The lower abdomen and core muscles are highlighted in red and yellow to demonstrate active muscular engagement while the straight legs are<br><br> raised and lowered in a slow, controlled movement.
</div>
</div>
<pre>
<h1>Step-by-Step How to Perform </h1>

<h3><b><u>1.Starting Position:</u></b></h3> Lie flat on your back on a mat with your legs extended straight out and feet together. Keep your arms flat on the floor beside your hips, palms facing down.
<h3><b><u>2.Set the Lower Back:</u></b></h3> Press your lower back down toward the floor to engage your core before moving your legs.
<h3><b><u>3.Lift the Legs:</u></b></h3> Keeping your legs straight (or with a subtle bend in the knees if your hamstrings are tight), exhale and lift your legs upward until they form roughly a 60° to 90° angle with the floor.
<h3><b><u>4.Lower with Control:</u></b></h3> Inhale as you slowly lower your legs back down toward the ground. Stop just a few inches before your heels touch the floor to keep constant tension on the abdominals.
<h3><b><u>5.Repeat: </u></b></h3>Control the movement at the bottom and begin the next rep without letting your legs rest on the floor.



 <h1>Benefits Of Exercise</h1>

<b>◉Targeted Lower Abs: Strongly targets the lower region of the rectus abdominis and deeper core stabilisers.</li>
  <b>◉Hip Flexor Strength:</b> Strengthens the iliopsoas and hip flexors, which aids in overall hip mobility and explosive leg power.</li>
  <b>◉Core Stability:</b> Helps train the abdomen to resist lower back arching, improving posture and performance in heavy compound exercises.</li>
  <b>◉Convenient:</b> Highly effective bodyweight exercise requiring no equipment.</li>



<h1> Reps, Sets & Recommendations</h1>

| Level | Reps Per Set | Sets | Rest Period |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Beginner | 8 – 12 reps | 3 Sets | 60 seconds |
| Intermediate | 12 – 15 reps | 3 – 4 Sets | 45 seconds |
| Advanced | 15 – 20+ reps | 4 Sets | 30 – 45 seconds |

>Tip:If your lower back arches off the mat as you lower your legs, place your hands underneath your glutes for support, or bend your knees to a 90° angle (Tuck Raises)<br> until your core strength improves.
